  "summary": "Washington's Senate passed SB 6002 on a 40-9 vote to regulate automatic license plate readers, capping data retention at 21 days (amended up from 72 hours), barring use for immigration enforcement and protest tracking, and prohibiting deployment near sensitive sites like schools and places of worship. The bill, sponsored by Sen. Yasmin Trudeau (D-Tacoma) and co-sponsored by Sen. Jeff Holy (R-Cheney), now moves to the House.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "For me right now, without this bill, anybody can access that information. It is literally the wild, wild west when it comes to data protection.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "Eight or more Washington law enforcement agencies seem to have allowed their reader networks to be shared with U.S. Border Patrol in 2025, per research conducted by the University of Washington's Center for Human Rights.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
